  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 AM MDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nLooks like a gradual ramp up to an active upcoming week with the\nbest monsoonal pattern we have seen this summer. For this weekend,\nwe will start out with a weak upper high near the International\nBorder with some better moisture over the far western areas where\nwe will have afternoon and early evening storms, focused on the NM\nBootheel and the Gila region moving east through Sierra County.\nVery localized heavy rain is the main threat. As the upper high\nshifts into the OK/AR area tomorrow and into the Lower Mississippi\nRiver Valley area by Mon, we start setting up a better moisture \npush with southeast winds bringing low level moisture from the\ngulf and more southerly mid/upper level winds. There is also an\napproaching upper trough moving into the west coast. Western zones\nwill have the best chances for storms, but both GFS and EC showing\noutflows pushing east and will likely trigger activity further\neast into the evening each day. Temperatures will remain about\n5-10 degrees above normal, but the far western zones could be\ncloser to normal depending on residual cloud cover.\n\nAs we go into Tue-Fri, things get more interesting. The\naforementioned upper trough meanders east and lingers around the\narea through this timeframe. A good moisture influx continues and\nPW's start to get into the 1.1-1.5\" range by the later half of the\nweek. Storm motions will mainly be under 10kts with a deep more\nsoutherly flow supporting training storms. With all of this in \nplace some locations will see several days of heavy rain which\ncould lead to some flash flooding. Things could change if this \ntrough pushes east quicker, but the upper high to the east will \nlikely not allow for that. Temperatures will also be dropping and \nbeing closer to normal or even below normal.",
